Y1  - 3-4 Sept. 2025
AB  - The need for efficient and high-performance computing systems have been driven by the growing demand for real-time image and video processing in applications such as autonomous vehicles, surveillance, augmented reality, and medical diagnostics. The need to generate these growing amounts of data has resulted in a new age where VLSI technology is regarded as a critical enabler for achieving the required computational power and energy efficiency in deep-learning-based applications. This paper performs a survey on how VLSI circuits can be integrated to perform deep learning tasks specifically targeting embedded systems that handle real-time processing of images and videos. VLSI plays a pivotal role in significantly improving the performance of executing the DNN operation execution, whether it is in the form of a CNN-based architecture or RNN architecture, while also addressing the trade-offs concerning speed, power consumption, and cost in the same process. An in-depth comparison of existing VLSI design paradigms and hardware architectures for real-time deep learning, focusing on efficient processing of large datasets on embedded systems. FPGAs are hardware platforms that can be programmed to implement a wide range of digital circuits, providing flexibility for custom neural networks, while ASICs are optimized for specific network architectures but lack reprogramability. Real-time video processing is discussed along with application examples of VLSI-based systems in different real applications, such as facial recognition, object detection, and scene segmentation. Due to the inherent similarities with current processing systems, the evaluation also includes a comparison against their traditional processing counterparts, demonstrating the transformative advancements in throughput and energy consumption achieved via VLSI. Lastly, their paper goes into detail on the hardware/software co-design, shortcomings of the memory bandwidth, and the parallelism in the architecture of the deep learning network. More specifically, the study points to the growing demand for significantly parallelized computing systems to handle the terabytes of data produced by image and video sensors. Furthermore, the analysis includes a discussion on the influence of deep learning model optimization methods like pruning, quantization, and hardware-aware training on VLSI hardware efficiency.
ER  - 

