AB  - The rollout of 5G networks presents a major opportunity for digital transformation in emerging economies, yet regulatory and policy challenges threaten to delay progress. This paper analyzes the current 5G regulatory landscape in emerging economies, focusing on spectrum allocation, licensing, institutional capacity, and regional policy coordination. Using qualitative policy analysis and case studies from South Africa, Nigeria, and Kenya, the study highlights disparities in readiness and identifies critical gaps. Key findings underscore the need for cohesive strategies to address infrastructure, governance, and inclusion. The paper offers policy recommendations, including spectrum reform, regional harmonization, and inclusive regulation, to support an enabling environment for 5G development in emerging economies.
