AB  - To address the issues of path redundancy, frequent turning, and low task allocation efficiency in multi-AGV path planning for automotive warehousing environments, this paper proposes a cooperative path planning method that integrates the Grey Wolf Optimizer (GWO) with a penalty-function-based A-star algorithm. First, a dynamic penalty function mechanism is introduced into the traditional A-star algorithm, where penalty weights are applied to AGV steering actions to effectively suppress unnecessary turns, thus improving path smoothness and operational efficiency. A bidirectional search strategy is also adopted to reduce computational complexity. Second, the multi-target task allocation problem is modeled as a Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP) and globally optimized using an enhanced Grey Wolf Optimizer. By incorporating a nonlinear convergence factor and a dynamic weight strategy, the algorithm’s ability to escape local optima is improved, allowing for rapid generation of the optimal task sequence. Experiments conducted on a warehouse map show that, compared with the traditional A-star algorithm, the proposed method offers significant advantages in path length, number of unnecessary turns, and overall task completion time. This method greatly enhances the transportation reliability, safety, and operational efficiency of AGV systems in automotive warehousing, providing effective technical support for the intelligent operation of high-density warehousing logistics.
ER  - 

