AB  - With the rapid expansion of high-dimensional image and video data across various domains—ranging from satellite surveillance and medical imaging to social media and industrial automation—the need for robust, scalable, and interpretable vision models has become critical. Traditional deep learning models, though powerful, often struggle to generalize across such vast data landscapes without significant computational costs and training inefficiencies. This paper proposes an optimized suite of deep vision models specifically designed for interpreting high-dimensional big data and enhancing visual understanding. The proposed framework incorporates advanced convolutional neural network (CNN) architectures, such as EfficientNet, Vision Transformers (ViTs), and hybrid attention-based models, and combines them with big data processing engines like Apache Spark, Kafka, and Hadoop. By adopting intelligent compression techniques, adaptive learning rate scheduling, and distributed training strategies, the models achieve both accuracy and scalability. The system also integrates dimensionality reduction methods such as t-SNE and PCA to mitigate the curse of dimensionality and improve model convergence. Experiments on large-scale datasets including Open Images, DeepLesion, and Places365 demonstrate the superior interpretability and efficiency of the proposed models. Key performance gains include reduced training times by up to 40%, enhanced classification accuracy by 5–8%, and improved generalization across unseen domains. Applications explored include real-time crowd behavior analysis, automated disease detection, and autonomous navigation. In summary, this work contributes an optimized deep vision modeling framework tailored for big data environments, enabling rapid, accurate, and interpretable visual analytics across high-dimensional data sources.
ER  - 

